{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1692361792/7/prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a6f40cea\" lines=\"74-88\">\n<description>\nComplete partial paths inside a framed rectangle by projecting external color traces inward.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a 1px rectangular frame (border color) with uniform interior fill.\n- Colored components outside the frame touching its edges.\n\nRule:\n- Output = the frame's interior, filled with the fill color.\n- Map each outside component's frame-touch points into interior coordinates.\n- Monochrome components become solid Manhattan stripes between mapped endpoints.\n- Two-color components become alternating-color Manhattan paths.\n\nKey insight: Endpoint colors of two-color traces anchor the alternating phase deterministically.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ecb67b6d\" lines=\"138-155\">\n<description>\nRecolor the grey diagonal-chain regions in an orange field to teal.\n\nInput structure\nThe input contains only grey pixels and orange pixels.  Grey pixels form\nseveral 8-connected regions embedded in the orange field.\n\nRule\nDecompose each grey 8-connected region into 4-connected lobes.  Recolor the\nwhole region teal only when it contains a straight diagonal run of at least\nthree pixels and those lobes meet only by diagonal corner contacts in a\nnon-branching diagonal chain or cycle; a single 4-connected region is\nrecolored only when it is a square diagonal staircase.\n\nKey insight\nThe teal regions are not selected by size or position; they are the grey\nregions whose internal connectivity depends on diagonal corner joins, while\nsame-edge cracks, rectangles, bars, and branching/noisy regions remain grey.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"dc46ea44\" lines=\"11-25\">\n<description>\nMove the lower sprite and its pink guide into the upper panel, aligning the sprite to the guide's lower-left branch.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one full-width separator row.\n- Below the separator are exactly two foreground roles: a five-pixel guide marker and one monochrome sprite.\n- The guide marker has normalized shape {(0,2), (1,2), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1)} and starts immediately below the separator.\n\nRule\n- Clear the canvas to the background, preserving the separator row.\n- Translate the guide marker upward so its top row becomes row 0.\n- Move the sprite, preserving its color and shape, so the sprite bbox bottom-right aligns to the translated guide's lower-left branch.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker supplies the destination anchor: its lower-left branch cell is where the moved sprite's bbox bottom-right must land.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"95755ff2\" lines=\"45-60\">\n<description>\nPaint straight rays from colored side markers into the hollow diamond frame until an interior foreground pixel blocks each ray.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains one complete one-pixel-thick diamond frame.\n- Colored marker pixels outside the diamond sit on the top, bottom, left, or right side of the frame's bounding box.\n- Any non-background, non-frame pixels inside the diamond are blockers that remain unchanged.\n\nRule\n- Each outside marker emits inward perpendicular to its side of the diamond bounding box.\n- Along that row or column, paint only background pixels strictly inside the diamond with the marker color.\n- Stop a ray when it reaches an existing interior foreground pixel; keep the frame and all original colored pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The diamond frame defines a strict interior, while the side markers are column or row emitters whose color fills the visible interior interval until blocked.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6ca952ad\" lines=\"15-30\">\n<description>\nDrop every large foreground object straight down to the bottom while leaving small marker objects fixed.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Objects of area at most three are stationary markers; larger objects are moving sprites.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each moving sprite from its original position.\n- Translate each moving sprite vertically downward until its bbox bottom reaches the grid bottom.\n- Repaint each translated sprite in its original color; small markers stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object area separates the active falling sprites from the small fixed markers, and all active sprites follow the same vertical path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"57edb29d\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nExtract the single plain rectangle and redraw it with the aligned rectangles' border-relative marker pattern.\n\nInput structure:\n- The boundary color is the background/separator.\n- Foreground objects are dense rectangular regions; exactly one rectangle has only its base color.\n- Other rectangles contain their base color plus marker pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop the only plain rectangle; this crop is the output canvas.\n- Use marked rectangles whose row span overlaps the plain rectangle's row span.\n- Transfer their marker pixels by preserving each marker run's distance from the nearest rectangle edges.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marked rectangles are examples of an edge-relative pattern, so the missing plain rectangle receives the same pattern resized to its own dimensions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nScale up a monochrome control sprite by stamping a polychrome tile at each filled pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background color.\n- Two foreground objects (8-connected): a monochrome rectangular sprite with bg holes (control), and a polychrome square tile (block).\n\nRule:\n- For each cell in the control sprite's bbox: if filled, stamp a copy of the block tile; if bg, leave a tile-sized bg region.\n- Output grid dimensions are (control_h × tile_h) by (control_w × tile_w).\n\nKey insight: The control sprite acts as a binary mask that gets \"pixel-scaled\" by the tile — each pixel expands to a full tile copy or empty space.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"332202d5\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nFill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.\n- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.\n- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.\n\nRule\n- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.\n- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.\n- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"98c475bf\" lines=\"62-76\">\n<description>\nErase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.\n- Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.\n- Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.\n\nRule\n- Keep the vertical frame unchanged.\n- Erase any complete interior glyph.\n- For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.\n\nKey insight\n- Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9720b24f\" lines=\"34-46\">\n<description>\nDelete each foreign-color object whose bounding box is contained by a larger object of another color.\n\nInput structure\n- The black pixels are the empty canvas, and every other color forms one or more 8-connected monochromatic objects.\n- Some larger foreground objects act as loose U/C-like containers around smaller objects of different colors.\nRule\n- Find all 8-connected monochromatic non-black objects.\n- Recolor an object to black exactly when its bounding box is contained in the bounding box of a larger object of another color.\n- Keep all other pixels unchanged.\nKey insight\n- The removable objects are identified by containment in another color's rectangular extent, not by their own color or shape.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2a28add5\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nPaint each pink control pixel as a teal horizontal bar whose reach equals the foreground counts on that row.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color and one or more pink control pixels.\n- Each marker row has exactly one pink pixel; all other non-background pixels\n  in that row are counters.\nRule\n- Start from an all-background output grid.\n- For each pink pixel, count non-background pixels strictly to its left and\n  strictly to its right in the same row.\n- Paint teal from c - left_count through c + right_count on that row.\nKey insight\n- The foreground pixels are not copied; only their counts on each side of\n  each pink marker encode the teal bar length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"668eec9a\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nSummarize the foreground line objects as ordered horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background fills a rectangular grid.\n- Each non-background color forms one contiguous one-cell-thick line segment.\n- The line segments have distinct topmost rows, and there are at most five of\n  them.\n\nRule:\n- Sort the foreground colors by the topmost row occupied by their line\n  segment.\n- Create a 5x3 output filled with the background color.\n- Place one solid 1x3 row for each foreground color, preserving that\n  top-to-bottom order and padding any unused rows above with background.\n\nKey insight: The exact line length and slope do not matter; only the\nvertical order of the colored line segments is encoded in the output stack.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"46c35fc7\" lines=\"21-34\">\n<description>\nRotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.\n\nRule\n- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.\n- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.\n- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"15\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6165ea8f\" lines=\"74-90\">\n<description>\nBuild a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.\n- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.\n- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.\n\nRule:\n- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.\n- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.\n- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.\n- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"16\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"af726779\" lines=\"17-35\">\n<description>\nGrow an alternating pink/orange triangular trace downward from the horizontal orange seed row.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a green background and one horizontal row containing all orange foreground pixels.\n- The seed row may contain isolated orange pixels and adjacent orange runs.\n- Pink is absent from the input and is used for the first generated row.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the input grid.\n- Two rows below the current active row, place a child pixel between each active pair\n  whose middle cell in the current row is background.\n- Alternate generated row colors between pink and orange, stopping when no children\n  can be generated or the next row would leave the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- Only an active-background-active triplet in the current row reproduces; contiguous\n  active runs do not produce children through occupied middle cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"17\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"18\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b5033c1\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nStraighten a colored snake path into a single-column strip preserving pixel order.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Non-bg pixels form a single 4-connected path (snake) with exactly two endpoints.\n- Each path pixel may be a different non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two endpoints (cells with only one path neighbor) of the snake.\n- Begin at the top-left-most endpoint and trace the path cell by cell.\n- Output an (N×1) column listing each cell's color in traversal order.\n\nKey insight: The path is a simple chain with no branches; choosing the top-left-most endpoint fixes a unique reading direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"19\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"11dc524f\" lines=\"25-40\">\n<description>\nSlide the non-square sprite to the square anchor and replace the anchor with the sprite's mirror image.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.\n- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.\n\nRule:\n- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.\n- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.\n- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.\n\nKey insight:\n- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"20\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83eb0a57\" lines=\"72-86\">\n<description>\nCrop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.\n- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.\n\nRule\n- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.\n- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.\n- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.\n\nKey insight\n- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n你的任务：\n以参考题为标杆，运用发散性思维进行创新扩展。请发挥创造力，构思全新且巧妙的推理谜题。用中文创造全新的 ARC 谜题描述。\n创新方式包括但不限于：\n**机制重组**：将多道参考题各自的核心机制提取出来，组合成一个新的多步管道，用到新的场景。\n**机制变异**：取几道参考题的核心机制，以机制为基础进行创新并与其他多种机制进行创新重组，使解题路径发生质变而非量变。\n**逆向构造**：取一道参考题的输出模式，思考\"什么样的输入规则能产生类似的输出结构但需要完全不同的推理过程\"。\n\n严格使用下面的 XML 风格格式输出：\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n请一次性生成5个`<new_puzzle>`，若有较多合适的创新，也可以生成更多，不设上限，但不要输出额外解释。\n\n注意事项：\n1. 任务不应依赖大量先验知识或复杂计算，而应依赖对模式、结构与空间关系的直觉推理。\n2. 关于\"计算\"的边界：\n   - **允许使用**（这些都是人类能心算的视觉量）：数像素 / 孔洞 / 对象 / 邻居（≤10 量级）、比大小、按某个可见属性排序、奇偶判断、取最大 / 最小、用一个计数值作为另一个等量几何量（如 N 个像素 → 长度 N 的射线、面积 N → 边长 N）、判断接触 / 相邻 / 包含、粗略中心位置（簇的视觉中点）、\"距离\"作为\"几步\"而不是公式等人类能够直观感受计算的内容。\n   - **禁止使用**（这些是算法量而非视觉量，会迫使解题者依赖公式而非视觉直觉）：曼哈顿 / 欧氏 / Chebyshev 距离作为精确查表键、凸包面积、二进制位编码 / 位运算、模运算（取模） / 余数、拓扑距离（BFS / 最短路 / 跳数）、精确几何质心坐标、凸角 / 凹角计数等复杂且不易被看出来的计算都是禁止使用的。\n3. 问题必须能从少量示例中推断出来（通常为 3 对训练样例 + 1 个测试样例）。设计时从人类解题者的视觉角度思考：规则涉及的所有关键要素（对象、关系、属性）必须在网格上肉眼可见，不应依赖人眼无法直接观察到的隐含信息（例如颜色id和颜色亮度是禁止使用的）或需要推算的隐藏属性。规则背后的逻辑本身可以有一定挑战性，但解题所需的所有线索必须能从网格中直接获取。\n4. 生成的问题描述旨在促进后续用于创建题目网格的代码生成。因此，对输入 / 输出网格的描述以及规则总结必须极其详细；语言风格应易于理解；对于特定对象（如箭头），必须在描述中明确其绘制方式。\n5. 描述谜题时避免使用 0 或 1 这类颜色编号；应使用颜色名称，例如 \"red\"。可用颜色如下：Black、Blue、Red、Green、Yellow、Grey、Magenta、Orange、Azure、Maroon。\n6. 新题的核心推理机制应明显不同——即从观察样例到归纳规则的认知路径不同，而不仅仅是场景、对象或颜色不同。\n7. 题目难度应来自机制的隐蔽性而非步骤数。用参考题作为难度标尺——如果你生成的题一个样例就能猜到规则，说明机制太透明，需要加深隐蔽性。\n8. 优先从参考题的机制出发进行重组或推演，而非从物理隐喻出发。物理隐喻容易导致大量题目归结为一种模式，例如\"沿方向移动到阻挡处停止\"。\n9. 题目的难度优先来自规则的**结构层数**，而不是单一步骤的复杂度。关于\"结构层数\"的判定标准：**只有解题者需要主动推理才能完成的步骤才算一层**。肉眼一扫即可获得的信息（如\"找到缺口在哪一侧\"、\"数出像素个数\"、\"识别哪个对象最大\"）不构成独立的推理层——它们只是变换的前置输入，不增加认知难度。真正的多层结构是指每一层都需要解题者发现一个非显然的规律。以两层结构为例：\n   - ✗ 伪两层：\"找到缺口方向\"→\"把东西推到对面\" — 第一步肉眼可见，实际只有一层推理。\n   - ✓ 真两层：\"通过孔洞数量匹配颜色图例\"→\"用匹配到的颜色重绘对象\" — 两步都需要非平凡的归纳。\n\n   如果你发现自己写出的题只是\"对每个对象，根据它的某个属性做某变换\"的单步规则，请引入一个需要主动推理的前置步骤。**用堆叠 tie-break 规则或复杂数学公式来\"加难度\"是劣解；用多一层需要归纳的结构嵌套来\"加难度\"才是好解**。\n10. 在符合ARC题目和提示词要求的基础上，鼓励尝试创新规则为多层结构的题目，不局限于两层，可以创新三层或四层结构的题目，但不可为了多结构层数违背要求。\n\n生成后对生成的题执行以下四项审查（审查内容不需要保存到文件）：\n1. 原创性\n  新题不可与上述参考题相同或类似，不可只是替换颜色、方向、对象类型等表层要素。判断标准：新题的核心推理路径应与任何一道参考题不同。\n2. 确定性\n  规则完全确定，同一输入只能产生唯一输出，每一步操作都有明确定义。\n3. 可实现性\n  所有必要细节都已给出——对象如何生成、如何识别、如何变换、边界情况如何处理，读完描述后可以直接写代码。\n4. ARC 合规性\n  网格尺寸合理（≤30×30），使用颜色不超过 10 种，规则复杂度适中，人类能从 3-5 个样例中发现规律。没有使用上述注意事项第二条禁止使用的复杂计算。\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"agent_message","text":"实现方案如下：\n\n1. 求解器会自己做 4-连通组件分解，给每个前景对象取紧致 `bbox` 和局部掩码。\n2. 配对逻辑不依赖生成器模板，而是按 `bbox` 尺寸分组，在同尺寸对象里寻找“两个局部掩码按位互补且并集恰好填满 bbox”的唯一配对。\n3. 输出时保留大碎片的原始 `bbox` 位置，画成实心矩形，颜色取小碎片颜色；小碎片原位置清空为黑色。\n4. 生成器会随机化：网格尺寸、碎片对数、每对 `bbox` 尺寸、局部切分形状、颜色、两碎片的相对位置，以及哪个碎片面积更大；同时用重试避免相邻粘连、避免歧义配对、避免越界和退化切分。\n5. 写入目标文件后我会运行题面给的 100 次自检和 `py_compile`，最后删掉验证产生的 `__pycache__`。"}}
